# Systems and Sensory Circuits
#
# A comparison of the senses by receptor, relay, and cortical target, then the motor loops, with ocular dominance as the anchor for sensitive periods.

#>neuroscience_systems
#:layer #!reference
#:domain #!neuroscience
#:form #!comparison
#:level #!advanced
#:review #!sourced
#:valence #!bone

^"emit"{
 ~#title: "Systems and Sensory Circuits"
 ~#claim: "Each sense turns one kind of energy into spikes at a dedicated receptor, sorts them through relays into maps, and lands in its own area of cortex; smell alone skips the obligatory thalamic relay."
 ~#proof: "Kuffler and Hubel and Wiesel for vision, von Bekesy for the cochlea, Penfield for the body map, Buck and Axel for smell, Zuker and Ryba for taste."
 ~#door: "Read across the matrix by sense, then down the columns by stage, then try the blind spot."
 ~#audience: "advanced students who know how a neuron fires"
 continuity: "Systems and Sensory Circuits | receptor | relay | cortex"
 cold_open: "Every sense follows the same route, receptor, relay, and cortex, except smell, which skips the usual relay."
}

